WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:10.860
In more ways than one, across two careers, Rick Fox has always been a defender.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.440
As an NBA player, he became known for his unrelenting physicality and sound defensive

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.440
play.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.000
He found the role his team needed him to adopt,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.520
(Commentary)

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.640
and he took a back seat to two of the biggest

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.060
stars the league has ever seen.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.760
But he carved out his own niche in esports, as well.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.460
He discovered it through a desire to improve the relationship with the son that his NBA

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.200
career had impacted.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.880
And when he eventually came out in support of esports, he again found himself forced

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.600
onto the defensive, and he embraced that role with enthusiasm.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:58.400
"I'm not here to debate the level at which they fit and slot in between basketball. They are digital athletes

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.040
they're professional digital athletes."

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:06.680
From a Bahamian high school to multiple NBA Finals and now playing an integral role in

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.360
esports’ future, this is Rick Fox’s story.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:19.800
Ulrich Alexander Fox was born on July 24, 1969 in Toronto, Ontario Canada but moved to his

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.080
father’s native Bahamas when he was young.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.120
In high school, it became clear that basketball would play a role in his future even though

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.840
he didn’t play in an organized environment until he was 14.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.760
In Nassau, at Kingsway Academy, Fox played for the Saints, but he also played basketball

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.430
in Warsaw, Indiana.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:44.479
And it was there that UNC Tar Heels coach Dean Smith first discovered Fox by accident

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:50.640
in 1986, as he attended a Warsaw game in Fox’s junior year with the intention of scouting

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.200
one of his opponents.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.440
"Do you remember when coach recruited you?"

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.880
"Yeah I do. It was back."

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.040
"Warsaw, Indiana."

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.360
Shortly after, Fox committed to the University of North Carolina starting in 1987, where

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.080
he studied Radio, Television and Motion Pictures.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.960
"Few players in Carolina history have combined the scoring, savvy, and defense that Fox displayed

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.800
during his star studded career."

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.920
But after helping his team reach the Final Four in 1991, Fox was drafted 24th overall

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.120
by the Boston Celtics in the 1991 NBA draft.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.960
While Fox did start on opening night, he played mostly from the bench during a transitional

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.460
year for the Celtics.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.100
Fox played with the Celtics during a rebuilding period that included their worst regular season

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:42.800
yet: a 15-67 record in 1996-97.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.180
"I was the captain of the Titanic, the year we won 15 games."

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.440
While Fox had become the team’s starting small forward and his steal numbers continued

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.620
to be among the NBA’s best, the team went a different direction.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.820
"It ended up being really fortunate because I bled green and didn't think I'd play anywhere else."

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.360
He landed with the Los Angeles Lakers, where he found himself playing alongside Shaquille

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.500
O’Neal and a young Kobe Bryant.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.160
(Commentary)

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:20.080
Fox started every game during the 1997-98 season, averaging 33 minutes per game.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:26.400
And it was in LA that he developed a reputation as a stable veteran presence, a role player

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.320
helping to moderate competing young players.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:36.380
"Phil commissioned me to do as the third captain. I was the go-between these guys. I was the go-between

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.980
coaching staff and the team."

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.120
The offense could run through Kobe and Shaq, while Fox focused on a strong defensive presence.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.200
(Commentary)

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.940
But there were moments when his team needed him: and Fox had a habit of coming through

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.600
in the clutch, whether from the 3-point line, or at the rim.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:09.780
(Commentary)

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:15.920
He also became known for protecting his teammates and physical play: usually on, but sometimes

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.240
off, the court.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:24.200
(Comentary)

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:30.220
Fox won three Championships with LA under legendary coach Phil Jackson, but a foot injury

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:36.540
saw him miss 40 games in 2003-2004, and he was traded to the Celtics at the end of that

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.000
season, where he decided to retire.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:46.380
During his NBA career, Fox had numerous acting credits to his name While some of those roles

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:52.660
were related to basketball, his most recognizable one during his NBA career was on HBO’s acclaimed

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.440
prison drama Oz.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.160
"Look pal, I don't do windows okay? I don't do windows."

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.280
After retiring from the NBA, Fox continued to act, making appearances in a number of

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:14.040
TV shows and films, and even cameoing with CollegeHumor’s comedic duo Jake and Amir.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.370
And in a lot of ways this seemed like a natural future for Fox, who had already told the media

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.920
that an acting career would be his goal post-NBA.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.400
And had he done only that, this video would not exist.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.860
But while Fox had been unrelenting as an athlete and actor up to this point, there was one

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.879
other role he was trying to balance: that of a father.

00:05:36.879 --> 00:05:42.400
Fox’s son Kyle, born in 1993, had lived on the East Coast while Fox had been with

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.480
the Lakers.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:48.320
But during Summers and on spring break, the younger Fox could visit Los Angeles.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.420
"He would say this to me, that basketball took Dad away."

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:57.100
"Sometimes it's frustrating, having a king for a dad. Cause he's always off doing stuff

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.120
on a very large scale."

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:04.560
Rick Fox said he was no stranger to video games, having received an Atari 2600 when

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.960
he was younger, and said he always planned to introduce games to his son.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.040
But it was when they discovered World of Warcraft that they found a game that they both loved,

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.040
and that bond helped them to connect even from across the continent.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.520
"But we started at, you were twelve years old. We got it at century city GameStop. I remember walking in

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:30.120
and looking and they had World of Warcraft, and I got it, we went home, we loaded on the computer.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.100
And instantly we were just in, we were in."

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.260
Their relationship helped Fox step into a

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.680
new arena — esports.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.940
Kyle introduced him to a MOBA called League of Legends, and, indulging his son’s interests,

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:48.220
Fox eventually arranged for both of them to visit Riot Games.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.600
The two were invited to attend the 2015 NA LCS Finals at Madison Square Gardens.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:59.020
"I expected to be there in support of my son’s passion, I expected to enjoy my time with

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.440
him, I didn’t expect to have a switch turned on."

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.360
"I didn’t expect to fall in love."

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:09.580
They were able to interact closely with Kyle’s favorite team, Counter Logic Gaming, and Fox

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.140
even gave them a pep talk.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:18.040
"Doesn't matter the sport, I came from a basketball world. And Kyle he's not basketball player.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:20.480
But he's a gamer, I try to tell him all the time it's no different."

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.540
Fox was not the only man with NBA ties that had taken notice.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:30.660
“I went upstairs to thank the owners of Riot Games for inviting us, and it was there that I ran into

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:36.280
Adam Silver, who I have a relationship with from my NBA days” In late 2015, these two

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:42.090
men were a step ahead of the flood of esports interest that had yet to come from celebrities

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:46.660
and sports organizations in 2016 and 2017.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:52.340
Fox and his son watched CLG took home the NA LCS championship and even followed the

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.200
team to Paris for Worlds.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.500
"Paris was a beginning.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:04.420
Being behind the curtain more with CLG, then I started to understand the business and through

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:09.620
and just a month later really through invitation to maybe be an advisor for CLG"

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.580
But Fox’s interest in esports had run beyond an advisory role.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:17.800
He had been an investor for some time, even trading stock trading tips in the locker room

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.280
with Shaq, and was open about his interest in startups.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.080
He had also already partnered with Twin Galaxies, which billed itself as the keeper of gaming’s

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:27.559
official world records.

00:08:27.559 --> 00:08:33.230
"I felt that if I was going to pour really a huge part of my time and energy into something,

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:35.599
I knew I was going to need to jump in."

00:08:35.599 --> 00:08:41.950
Fox purchased Gravity Gaming’s NA LCS spot in December 2015 and announced that the team

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.160
had been rebranded as Echo Fox.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.360
"Actually getting that slot built and moving into ownership title, I remember the day.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:57.280
I remember the actual shift in my body. Because it instantly generated inside of me a

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.180
an anxiety that I didn't think existed before."

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.940
But as they were ready to take the stage for their first ever game, visa issues meant that

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:11.460
Echo Fox couldn’t field their full roster in Week 1 of the 2016 NA LCS Spring Split.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:16.080
Those issues persisted and a number of roster changes over the course of the split made

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.660
things difficult.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.340
"Meanwhile Echo Fox are losing four straight for themselves they gotta turn this one around and

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.340
fast. They haven't a game since opening day."

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:38.000
(Casting)

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.300
"Last week Fox dropped to 9th place and continue to struggle with a lineup filled out with subs.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:47.260
from what we've seen which leaves fans wondering.   Is there a Froggen at the end of the tunnel."

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:55.820
"I didn't expect to put out as many fires as we put out. And went through the first four weeks of

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:02.680
their season. Some cases not even together. They still practice. They weren't even allowed to compete.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:08.540
There was so much that went on there. That to have survived to have make it to seventh?

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.920
Like I stand up and applaud."

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.040
Despite fewer roster issues that summer, their results were even worse:

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.280
"Echo Fox has probably been the biggest surprise for me this split and not for a good reason."

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:28.360
(Casting)

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:33.920
"Definitely looks bad for Echo Fox, they are struggling still. It's been kind of the story for them for this split

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.520
though really rough split for them."

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:41.280
Tenth place after a dismal 1-17 record, and a trip to the promotion tournament

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:44.160
where they beat NRG to retain their spot.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:50.400
(Casting)

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.740
He was one of the first people from the traditional sports world to dive into a major esports

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:04.960
venture, and among those who did in late 2015, he was the most immediately recognizable.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:09.280
"But in that time since that split we have a CS:GO team. We have a Call of Duty team.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:15.940
We have H1Z1 expression that we're flushing out and scouting for our four pros that'll represent us there.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:22.520
We have a Street Fighter, Julio. We have Mew2King in Smash. We keep evolving and building

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:24.120
our organization of players."

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:29.420
But in many cases, the world was unaware of esports, and the mainstream media often found

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.320
the concept laughable.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:35.760
"So this is basically going to be a League watching people play video games."

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.500
"And I'm laughing because I can't think of anything less interesting."

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:46.000
That meant Fox became esports’ de-facto defender in the world of traditional sports

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:46.820
and entertainment.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:53.880
"When do you think this can really become bigger than an actual professional sport like football, baseball, MLB."

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.640
"It’s in that conversation now, it’s just fifth.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.100
And so now, it's just fifth."

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:01.720
"Your fifth place?"

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:03.460
"we're in fifth place and it's coming."

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.080
"How many years, give me a real estimate?"

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:07.300
"Two years it'll be on par with the NHL."

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:11.460
And he wasn’t just defending his investment as something likely to pay off.

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:16.040
Fox argued that the players shouldn’t be ashamed, and that they counted as athletes,

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:19.000
at a time when many found the idea controversial.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:27.180
"I’m not here to debate the level at which they fit and slot in between basketball … they are digital

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.430
athletes, they are professional digital athletes."

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:36.420
"A lot of people know I stand with the athletes as professional athletes, and I’ll debate

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:41.640
that with anybody I'll have that conversation with anybody." "I think the trigger point is professional…"

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:49.440
"And before you shun or impose your idea of what you think someone else should be doing  … check yourself."

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:53.620
But while Fox and his organization were ahead of the curve, others had joined the party

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:55.440
in 2016.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.560
This included Fox’s old teammate Shaq,

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:03.100
"I asked him do you know much about esports, he said, "Nah what is all this." And I said, "We should talk."

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:08.420
and go "I own a franchise" and I said, "I think it's something that would be worth your time,

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:10.400
your effort, your energy, your focus."

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:20.680
I was so thrown in the day to day of getting Echo Fox off the ground I think I slipped in following up with him

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:26.980
And Andy Miller from the NRG got in there through the ownership of the Sacramento Kings which Shaq is a

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:27.760
member in.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:35.140
And pulled him to that side. We'd gone from obviously great teammates to great rivals in esports."

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:38.420
"How soon and how quick it shifts."

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:45.420
"Hey Rick Fox, you know me. NRG beat your team's ass the other day. And this, is the legend they call

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:45.920
Sniffy."

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:53.020
"You suck. See this Ricky? This is what two legends look like. Rick, you and your team suck."

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:59.300
2016 had been an entry for Rick Fox, but none of his teams or players had found consistent

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:00.800
competitive success.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:04.700
And in League of Legends, Fox’s investment faced a turning point.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:10.640
An interview featuring Team SoloMid owner Andy “Reginald” Dinh about the risk that

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:17.250
relegation posed to investment was the spark that lit a wildfire discussion about the profitability

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:22.940
of League of Legends: partially because sweeping game changes can instantly affect a team’s

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:23.820
chances.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.080
“But from an owner perspective and a player perspective, it's honestly really discouraging."

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:35.300
"In order to make an easy example I would probably say it's like - You look at like the NBA right, where they go

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:39.100
into like the NBA playoffs, it would be essentially changing the basketball-

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:43.140
basketball's weight, and changing it into like shooting a bowling ball instead of a basketball.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.460
If the meta can change that quickly, then

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:51.340
an owner’s ability to hold onto their NA LCS spot can change just as rapidly.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:56.220
After a somewhat tone-deaf response from Riot co-founder Marc “Tryndamere” Merrill on

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:00.300
Reddit so-called “LCS Forever” movement began.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:06.220
A letter to Riot from almost all of the EU and NA teams was published by Slingshot Esports

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:12.440
in November 2016, and it detailed the teams’ demands for financial stability including

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.520
an end to relegation.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:20.120
Every team with a spot in both the NA and EU LCS had a spot where they could sign the

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:20.620
letter.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:23.500
Every team except Echo Fox.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:28.480
Another story claimed that the organization had attempted to poach a Phoenix1 player still

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:34.940
under contract, and that the other NA LCS teams would refuse to scrim Echo Fox as a result.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:39.480
Riot later claimed that because the player’s contract hadn’t been updated in the internal

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.600
roster tracker, that no harm had been committed.

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:49.080
Heading into Spring 2017, Echo Fox did indeed have trouble finding scrims in the NA LCS.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:53.360
And they would finish eighth in both the Spring and Summer splits after playing around with

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:55.360
the idea of a 10-player roster.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:58.560
"We don’t really scrim LCS teams because they don’t want to scrim us

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:04.100
It's definitely holding us back, I mean if we can't scrim the best competition it's harder for us to improve."

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:09.560
But in April 2017, when theScore esports broke the news that franchising would come to the

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:15.760
LCS in 2018, Fox made it clear that his organization was gunning for a spot.

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:23.040
"Yeah I would say it's safe to say we're beyond interested. We've been a part of the LCS now for two

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:28.960
years and four splits, we've now figured out our footing when it comes to the competitive side,

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:37.000
But as for our commitment to wanting to be part of this partnership moving forward between Riot

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:43.200
and selections they make in coming months here. We are definitely submitting an application and

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.200
and we look forward to seeing and hearing the results."

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:51.500
And in the end, Echo Fox were one of the teams selected for a permanent spot in the league,

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:57.300
Simultaneously, Echo Fox did the nearly unthinkable: they signed seven FGC

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:04.940
players in January 2017: Justin Wong, Tokido, Momochi, SonicFox, MKLeo,

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:06.780
Scar and Chocoblanka.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:14.240
Easily the biggest single roster move in FGC history, even if a former teammate was jokingly less than

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:15.060
impressed."

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:22.620
"I'm here to talk about this Echo Fox stuff on Twitter. I don't know what these guys think they doing."

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:28.120
"I don't know who they think they are, but uhh... You know they're owned by Rick Fox.

00:17:28.120 --> 00:17:31.100
so I know who he thinks they are. I know who he thinks."

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:36.960
But in July, Tokido went on to win EVO 2017, the biggest fighting game tournament in the

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:39.100
world, with his iconic Akuma.

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:50.520
(Casting)

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:55.840
"You guys know from my face, I am so happy now."

00:17:55.840 --> 00:18:00.960
"You know you came all the way from Japan. Is there anybody at home or is there anybody that you wanna

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.140
give a shoutout to?"

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:11.200
"Yes. Just one thing."

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:12.780
"I wanna say."

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:18.920
"Fighting game is something so great."

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:29.140
And a wave of new esports investors, some of whom had been wary of the potential of fighting

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.400
games, saw it as well.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:39.840
2018 has so far seen Echo Fox have their most competitive split ever in the NALCS.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:19:13.180
(Casting)

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:15.080
"This is why this is a playoff team."

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:19.080
Earning their first playoff berth in the Spring and finishing third.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:32.480
(Casting)

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:37.679
For an organization that hadn’t finished above seventh in their entire LCS tenure,

00:19:37.679 --> 00:19:42.399
third was a promising improvement, and one that validated some of the struggles of the

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:43.760
previous two years.

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:51.840
"It's been an amazing three years growing as an organization. But being welcomed into this community

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:58.960
by all of you, and watching our brand grow. The beauty of it is that as exciting it is right now here

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:06.280
it's the Spring Split. This thing builds and builds builds, summer's right around the corner

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:12.080
And I can't wait, we're still trying to make it to Worlds, and we still wanna win a World Championship.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:14.780
So we're not going anywhere."

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:21.580
"If you don't set the goal of becoming a champion, then you'll never move towards that."

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:28.360
"If your goal is to just not be relegated. You'll move towards just not being relegated.

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:32.340
If you're goal is to just make playoffs , you'll move to just making the playoffs.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:40.060
If your goal is to become a champion, and you head down that path as an individual,

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:47.140
And as a team? You will move towards becoming a champion. Now I can't promise you when that will

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:52.340
reveal itself, when it will show up. I can't promise if it'll happen with us.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:56.980
But what I know I want for each of our players is I want them to be champion. Why? I know what it feels like

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:59.740
to be champion. I know what that feels like, it is incredible.

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:01.960
It is something that no one can ever take away from you."

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:10.760
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

